Revision history for Term::Fabulous

0.01    Unreleased
        - Input validation. Every input widget takes required (an empty
          value, an unchecked checkbox or a dropdown without a selection
          is invalid), required_message and validator: the name of a
          built-in validator (email, integer, number, url, hostname, ip,
          date, time), a regular expression, a code reference, a list of
          those, or a Term::Fabulous::Validator with options (integer
          min/max, a message of your own). The inputs gain error,
          is_valid and validate, fire the new event
          Term::Fabulous::Event::ValidityChange after a Change whose
          message changed, and show an invalid value in the new theme
          state invalid (input.text and input.border.color default to
          the danger token; the new invalid_color parameter overrides the
          text). Term::Fabulous::Widget's invalid_inputs lists the
          invalid inputs below a widget. The role behind it is
          Term::Fabulous::Role::Validatable.
        - The text inputs take accept, which restricts the characters the
          user may type and paste: the body of a character class (what
          KDL writes), a regular expression per grapheme cluster or a
          code reference; rejected clusters are dropped, the program's
          value may not contain one. A validator that knows its
          characters (integer, number, date, time) suggests an accept
          spec that applies unless the input has one of its own.
          Term::Fabulous::Editor holds it as accept and set_accept.
        - Links in Term::Fabulous::Widget::RichText: ranges of the text
          with a target, from [link=TARGET]...[/link] markup or from the
          links parameter and add_link (any value is a target). They are
          painted over the spans in the new theme slots text.link and
          text.link.background (normal, hovered, selected). A click on a
          link, or Enter on the selected one, fires the new event
          Term::Fabulous::Event::LinkActivate. A RichText with links is a
          Tab stop (can_focus => 0 opts out) whose Left and Right select
          the previous and next link. Term::Fabulous::Text::Markup's
          parse_markup returns the links as a third value.
        - Term::Fabulous::Event::TextClick: a mouse button pressed on a
          character of a Text or RichText fires it on the text, after the
          Mouse event that still goes to the widget behind it, with the
          character's offset, its word, and the spans and link there.
          Term::Fabulous::Widget::Text composes the event emitter role
          and gains click_at, so a Text may also be the root widget.
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Sixel: a subclass of Image with the same
          sources and fits, whose picture the terminal draws in its own
          pixels as sixel graphics over the widget's cells. Its natural
          size is the cells its pixels cover. Cells painted over it
          (dialogs, dropdowns, toasts, floating boxes) are cut out of the
          picture, a ScrollBox shows it only in its visible cells, and it
          never covers the terminal's last row. It needs Imager and
          Imager::File::SIXEL (recommended, not required) and a terminal
          that shows sixel graphics and reports its cell size; otherwise
          it shows a notice in its place.
          Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Termbox asks the terminal for sixel
          support and its cell size when it opens and after a resize. New
          optional cell target role Term::Fabulous::Render::Target::Sixel,
          composed by Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Termbox::Cells and
          Term::Fabulous::Render::Target::Grid;
          Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Memory takes sixel_cell_size.
          Term::Fabulous::Termbox gains tf_sixel_query, tf_cells_differ and
          tf_invalidate_cells, Term::Fabulous::Widget::Canvas the
          sixel_data hook and Term::Fabulous::Render::Frame painted_over.
          tools/screenshot emulates a sixel terminal with 10x20 pixel cells;
          tools/mandelbrot draws the example picture of the Sixel programs
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Image: a picture read with Imager from a
          file, bytes, base64 or base64url text or a data URL, drawn in
          half-block pixels, two per cell. Its natural size is the
          picture's, and it keeps that size in a larger or smaller
          widget unless fit (contain, stretch) scales it.
          Transparent pixels show the background, translucent ones are
          mixed with it. Imager is a recommended, not a required,
          dependency: without it the widget shows a notice in its place.
          New theme family image with the slot notice
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Termbox's tb_cluster_width,
          tb_set_cell_ex, tb_set_cell and tb_extend_cell wrote past their
          codepoint buffer and never returned when given a string that
          carries the UTF8 flag over malformed bytes (Encode::_utf8_on, a
          :utf8 read layer); they die "needs well-formed UTF-8" now, and
          Term::Fabulous::Unicode's sanitize_text dies with a
          Term::Fabulous message instead of Perl's "Malformed UTF-8
          character (fatal)"
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous's run called from inside a running run (a
          listener or a timer) closed the terminal under the outer run,
          removed its timers and input watcher, and left IO::Async's
          handlers for SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP in %SIG for good; such a
          call dies now ("run is active already; it cannot be called again
          until it returns") and leaves the active run as it was
        - Fixed: a Term::Fabulous::Theme accepted 'none' (or undef, #null)
          for slots whose widgets cannot draw without a value, and any
          border style for tabs.line.style, and the first frame then died
          deep inside the widget (a Perl dereference error in the
          scrollbar, a tab bar or table part rejecting undef). These slots
          are required now: scrollbar.track and scrollbar.thumb,
          tabs.line.style, input.star and input.inactive, table.text,
          table.header.text, table.cursor, table.muted, table.line.color
          and table.pager.button, accordion.title, accordion.accent and
          accordion.disabled, and the text, important_text and kind colors
          of toast. 'none' for them dies where the theme is built ("SLOT
          cannot be 'none'"), and tabs.line.style must be a style with
          joints
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::VirtualList stopped OnScroll at
          the list (its internal listener returned undef), so listeners on
          its ancestors never saw a scroll of the list; it bubbles on like
          the OnScroll of a ScrollBox
        - Fixed: after Term::Fabulous::Widget::VirtualList's count shrank
          and grew again, the new items at the old indices were counted
          with the measured heights of the dropped items until each was
          shown, so the content height, the scrollbar and scroll_to_item
          were off
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Enum::BorderStyle's junction dropped a
          whole axis when the arm it looked at first had no joints (Hidden,
          Blank, ...), although the opposite arm had them, and drew a
          straight line where a corner or a T belongs
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::XYChart's box line marker drew the
          part of a line outside the plot (points before a fixed x min or
          the span, after a fixed x max) over the labels of the y axis and
          right of the plot, and walked every column of that part; it
          draws in the columns of the plot only
        - Fixed: lines of Term::Fabulous::Widget::XYChart (LineChart,
          AreaChart, Sparkline, ...) took time with the square of their
          points (4000 points: a second per frame), and an area with a
          span or a fixed x range filled every subpixel column of its whole
          history (a span of 60 over 1001 points: 9 s per frame). The point
          under each part of a line is found by halving the sorted points,
          and lines and areas are made only where they reach into the plot
          (with the shape the whole curve has there), so they draw the same
          in a fraction of the time
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Chart::Scale::Linear's fit with a step far
          too small for the data (y_axis => { step => 1 } over millions)
          listed every multiple before giving up on it, seconds per frame;
          such a step is skipped at once, and the axis shows its two ends
          as before
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Chart::Scale::Log's fit ignored a fixed max
          below the data and ran the axis over the data; the max is kept
          and the min moves a power of the base below it, as Linear does
        - Fixed: the hover labels of a chart on a category axis copied all
          labels for every point (4000 categories: 1.5 s per frame);
          Term::Fabulous::Chart::Scale::Category gains label_at
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Chart::Curve's natural curve died
          "Illegal division by zero" for three or more points at one x
          (repeated samples), and with it every frame of a LineChart or
          AreaChart with curve => 'natural' and such data; a point between
          two others at its x is held straight like the ends, and the curve
          through points at distinct x is unchanged
        - Fixed: the min and max of an XYChart axis (LineChart, BarChart,
          Sparkline, Histogram, ...) were checked only when a frame was
          drawn, so a log axis from 0, a min not below the max or a time
          axis from no date died in the first frame after the change; they
          die when the axis is given now, naming the axis and the end,
          also for the ends of a linear x axis and of an automatic one,
          which take a number or a date
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::RadarChart accepted a min not
          below its max, and its first frame died; that dies when it is
          given, also through the min and max accessors
        - Fixed: a Term::Fabulous::Widget::Histogram series accepted
          [ x, y ] points and its frame died; they die when they are given
          (constructor, set_data, add_points, append), as documented, and
          the data stays as it was
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::Histogram made as many bins as a
          bin_width asked for (0.01 over 0 .. 10000: a million bins, 12 s
          per frame) and took any number of bins; a histogram has at most
          1000 bins now: bins dies above 1000, and a bin_width too small for
          the data widens to the smallest round width that needs at most
          1000 bins
        - Fixed: removing or hiding the series (or the pie, donut or polar
          area slice) under the mouse pointer left the chart faded and
          hovered() reporting it until the pointer moved; the hover ends
          as if the pointer had moved off it, with a SeriesHover event
          without a series
        - Fixed: an unnamed series added after another was removed could
          get the name of a series still there ("Series 2" twice) and died;
          it takes the next free "Series N"
        - Fixed: append changed the series before the one whose value was
          invalid, so the series fell out of step; every value is checked
          before any series changes, as documented
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::RadarChart drew no points for
          point => 'dot' and point => 'square'; they are Braille dots at
          the corners now, as on the XY charts
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::PieChart (and DonutChart,
          PolarAreaChart) made with other_label => undef labeled the
          folded slice with nothing and warned; undef means 'Other', as
          for the other_label accessor
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::Table kept showing the old cell
          texts and data-dependent styles of a row given again with an id
          it had shown: rows() with the same row_id values (a monitor that
          refreshes its rows every second showed the first values for
          good), or a row removed and added again with its id before the
          next frame. A row the model stores starts at the model's
          revision now, so its revision always differs from that of the
          row shown before
        - Fixed: Term::Fabulous::Widget::Table's remove_rows (and
          Table::Model's) took time quadratic in the number of rows:
          removing 10000 of 20000 rows took 21 s, it takes 0.04 s now
        - Fixed: every selection change of a Term::Fabulous::Widget::Table
          walked and sorted all rows (about 55 ms per Space press on 20000
          rows); the data order is kept until rows are set, added or
          removed, and select and deselect sort only the rows they change
        - Fixed: tools/screenshot died ("unsupported SGR parameters") on a
          printed report with dim, italic, blink, concealed, struck out or
          overlined text, which Term::Fabulous::Static writes; it reads
          every style Static writes now
        - Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Termbox closes an open session when it
          is destroyed, also when the program ends or dies, so a program
          that opened the terminal through step or open and died outside
          run no longer leaves the shell in raw mode, on the alternate
          screen and with mouse reporting on
        - Term::Fabulous::Layout's new takes allowed_namespaces, a list of
          package names a layout may load modules from (the namespaces and
          the modules below them); a use of any other module dies before
          anything of the layout is loaded. Without it, any module loads
          as before
        - A Term::Fabulous::Theme file that sets a palette token twice, or a
          slot of a family, state or variant twice, dies naming the token
          or slot instead of keeping the last value
        - Term::Fabulous::Chart::Curve's curve_points takes between =>
          [ first, last ], which makes only the part of the polyline from
          one point to another, with the shape of the whole curve there
        - Term::Fabulous::Chart::Series's add_points comes in two steps,
          parsed_points (parse and check, store nothing) and push_parsed
          (store), for callers that change several series at once
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Chart gains end_hover_unless_shown, which
          a chart calls after it took series or slices away
        - Term::Fabulous::Render::Attr exports STYLE_FLAGS, the one table of
          the style flags with their SGR parameters and names; the
          ::Render::Target::Grid row_text and the screenshot tools both
          use it, and row_text writes 21 for TB_UNDERLINE_2, as termbox2
          does
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget, ::Widget::Text and ::Widget::VirtualList
          react to a new place in a tree through Clay::UI's tree_changed
          hook, which reaches every widget of a moved subtree, instead of
          overriding Clay::UI's private _set_parent and _detach_parent: a
          VirtualList is prepared again also when a subtree holding it
          moves, each widget forgets only its own looks, and
          Term::Fabulous::Role::Themed's forget_looks and forget_tree_looks
          walk the tree once (Clay::UI's descendants)
        - Fixed: Tab and Shift+Tab inside an open
          Term::Fabulous::Widget::Dialog skipped the focusable items of a
          VirtualList (and every widget below an internal child). The
          backdrop steps with Clay::UI's default order limited to the
          dialog (default_next_focus( within => $dialog )), and its
          focus_order method is gone
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget's find_by_id and RadioGroup's buttons walk
          the tree in layout order (Clay::UI's descendants), so they also
          find the widgets below an internal child, such as the items of a
          VirtualList
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget documents descendants and contains, the
          subtree queries every widget inherits from Clay::UI
        - The layout, floating, border_width and other Clay attributes
          read back with snake_case keys whatever spelling was set
          (Clay::UI stores them that way), so a VirtualList given
          childGap or childAlignment in camelCase uses them, and a
          RadioGroup reads only layout_direction
        - Term::Fabulous::Render::Geometry gains line_steps, the one
          clipped Bresenham stepper: PixelCanvas's draw_line and the chart
          raster's lines step through it (same pixels as before)
        - Term::Fabulous::Role::HasBorderStyle's border_style_of($side)
          answers the style a side is drawn in: its own, else the one the
          widget derives (the new optional hook derived_border_style), else
          the theme's border.style, else Blank. The renderer and the Hidden
          side rule read it, and the border_style_top/right/bottom/left
          readers return it for every widget (they returned undef for a
          side without a style of its own). Tabs::Button, Tabs::Page and
          the dropdown's option list derive their borders through the hook.
          Fixed: a style given to a tab's side was ignored
        - Term::Fabulous::Role::Themed owns how looks change: a widget
          declares each themed parameter once, with its kind (color,
          cell_color, optional_color, border_style, ...), next to its slot
          in themed_params, and the role takes it from the constructor,
          checks every value and makes it a layout property;
          adopt_look_params is gone. One optional hook, looks_changed,
          hears every change (set_look, reset_look, a theme switch, joining
          a tree in a UI) and replaces theme_changed. forwarded_looks
          declares looks a widget keeps on its parts: reset_look now
          returns the colors and line style of a Tabs (kept by its bar) and
          the track_color and thumb_color of a ScrollBox (kept by both
          scrollbars) to the theme (it died). A color parameter given
          undef dies with a message that names reset_look
        - Dropdown takes disabled options, in the option grammar of
          SegmentedControl ({ label => ..., disabled => 1 }, and
          'option "X" disabled=#true' in KDL): shown in the disabled
          color, skipped by the keys and typing, not chosen by a click;
          the program may still select one. Term::Fabulous::OptionList
          holds the options and the selection of both widgets, and
          Term::Fabulous::Role::HasOptions reads their KDL options.
          Term::Fabulous::Roving is the one rule for moving between
          entries with the keys (wrap or clamp, disabled entries skipped),
          used by SegmentedControl, Dropdown, Tabs, Tabs::Bar, RadioGroup
          and Accordion
        - Term::Fabulous::Range holds the value of a Slider, a StarRating
          and a ProgressBar: one range check, one snapping rule, one key
          table, one value format; Term::Fabulous::Role::HasRange applies a
          layout's range properties together for all three. StarRating
          gains set_range( max => ..., half => ... )
        - Term::Fabulous::Viewport: the arithmetic of a scrolled view
          (max_offset, clamp_offset, reveal_range, scroll_thumb), used by
          the Scrollbar, the dropdown list, TextArea's view and the table.
          Every scrollbar is painted by Scrollbar's paint_track, so the
          scrollbars of a TextArea and of a dropdown list are drawn in the
          theme's scrollbar colors (were the input's disabled and accent
          colors). Role::Themed gains family_look for such parts
        - The charts (Term::Fabulous::Widget::Chart), the Scrollbar and
          the dropdown's option list are Term::Fabulous::Widget::Display
          widgets: they paint through Display's paint key, so a theme
          switch now repaints a chart (it kept the old ink), and a chart
          or scrollbar without a sizing takes its natural size (a chart
          grows, a scrollbar is one cell thick). Chart's revision is gone
        - One rule for the color a widget lies on:
          Term::Fabulous::Widget's background_below (its own or the
          nearest opaque ancestor background, else the screen color of the
          theme, else none). Canvases, tables and charts use it, so under
          the light theme table cells and chart ink follow the screen
          (were dark cells on a light screen and an invisible chart
          title); dark-theme tables and charts on no background of their
          own now lie on the dark screen color too. Static output is
          unchanged. Term::Fabulous::Render's screen_background_attr is
          gone
        - Every color takes the 148 CSS color names in any case
          ('steelblue', 'SteelBlue'): widget color parameters and
          accessors, canvas cells, KDL layouts and theme files, not only
          rich text markup; Term::Fabulous::Color parses them, and
          Term::Fabulous::Text::Style uses it instead of a table of its
          own. Term::Fabulous::Text::Style gains compose_styles, the one
          rule for stacking styles, which RichText uses for nested spans;
          the renderer paints each run with apply_style
        - Border styles are taken as a Term::Fabulous::Enum::BorderStyle
          item or its name everywhere a style is taken: border_style and
          border_style_top/right/bottom/left of every widget (constructor
          and accessors), KDL, Divider and Tabs line_style, table lines
          and theme slots. Term::Fabulous::Check gains one_of,
          border_style, value_format and optional, and every widget
          checks its words, border styles and value formats with them,
          so the messages have one shape: 'NAME must be one of A, B, C,
          got X' (sorted) and 'NAME must be a border style or its name,
          got X (known: ...)'. A KDL null is reported as undef
        - Children are removed by identity: remove_child takes the widgets
          themselves (also Text widgets and widgets without an id) and
          dies for an id, remove_child_with_id removes children by id and
          has_child tells whether a widget is a direct child; from
          Clay::UI's Container. Tabs, Tabs::Bar, Accordion::Item and
          Toast forward both removals to their pages, tabs or body
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::RichText: a Text whose spans give ranges
          of it bold, italic, underline, reverse, dim, blink, strike,
          overline, conceal, a color and a background, from markup in the
          syntax of Python's rich ('[bold red]Esc[/]') or as [start, end,
          style] spans and stylize(). Clay lays the text out as before and
          the renderer reapplies the spans per wrapped line, which needs
          the stringOffset of Clay::XS 0.05. Term::Fabulous::Text::Style
          parses style strings, Term::Fabulous::Text::Markup the markup;
          Static output now emits the SGR codes of every style bit.
        - Themes: Term::Fabulous::Theme colors every widget that is not
          given its own colors or border styles, from a palette of
          tokens and the slots of each widget family, with variants
          selected by a widget's classes. Built-in themes dark and
          light; themes written in KDL theme files (from_file) or in
          Perl; set per UI with theme => ... and switched at run time
          with $ui->theme(...); reset_look returns a given color to the
          theme. A Text without a text_color is now drawn in the
          theme's text color instead of black. Widget authors: the
          Term::Fabulous::Role::Themed role (theme_family,
          themed_params, look, look_value, theme_changed); a Button's
          disabled_text_color became child_text_color. Term::Fabulous
          paints the screen in the theme's background token before every
          frame, so a theme looks the same on any terminal (a token with
          alpha 0 keeps the terminal's own background; Static paints
          none; the dark theme's background is #161622, and the example
          programs no longer give their root a background of their own).
          Borders without a color are drawn in the theme's border
          token instead of the terminal's default text color, and a
          widget with a border_width but no border_style gets the
          theme's style, Round in the built-in themes (was spaces). The theme files
          moved to examples/themes/, with nord, dracula and
          solarized-light beside ocean
        - Needs Perl 5.32.1 or later (was 5.24)
        - Term::Fabulous::Layout needs Text::KDL::XS 0.002, which reads a
          string as Perl characters; layouts given as a string are passed
          to it as they are, layout files as a UTF-8 filehandle
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::ScrollBox shows scrollbars: a vertical
          one in the last column inside the border and, while it scrolls
          sideways, a horizontal one in the last row; the content keeps
          clear of them, a click or a drag on them scrolls, scrollbar => 0
          removes them, track_color and thumb_color color them (also in
          KDL). The table's scrollbar is now the general
          Term::Fabulous::Widget::Scrollbar (was ::Table::Scrollbar), for
          either axis, which handles its own clicks and drags and is laid
          out zero cells thick for an axis its container does not scroll
          along; position_at replaces position_at_row. Needs Clay::UI's
          internal children (add_internal_children, layout_children)
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Tabs and ::Tabs::Page: pages behind a
          row of tabs that joins the page's border, the active tab one
          cell larger and open toward the page; the bar on any side, with
          horizontal or downward labels, the tabs at the start, center or
          end, in any border style with joints, with or without a border
          around the page, disabled tabs, the keys (arrows, Home, End,
          Ctrl+PageUp, Ctrl+PageDown), the mouse, Select events and KDL
          properties; Term::Fabulous::Widget::Tabs::Bar and
          ::Tabs::Button are the bar and its tabs, usable on their own
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Toast: a notification of a kind (info,
          success, warning, danger) with an icon, a title, a message and a
          close mark that show($ui) floats into a corner of the screen,
          stacked below the toasts there, and that goes away after a
          timeout, with the close mark or hide, firing Close; important
          toasts are filled with their color, and a toast added to the
          layout is an alert box
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Accordion and ::Accordion::Item: sections
          that open and close under their headers, one at a time or
          several, with the keyboard (Enter, Space, Up, Down, Home, End)
          and the mouse, disabled items, borders, the toggle at the start
          or the end, and KDL properties; the new
          Term::Fabulous::Event::Select reports what the user opened or
          closed
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Spinner: shows that something is going
          on, in twelve ready-made styles of one cell to three rows or
          with frames of your own, with a label, start and stop, and KDL
          properties; it runs on the application's clock
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::ProgressBar: how much of a task is done,
          in block, line and ASCII styles with eighth-cell precision, a
          label left, right or inside the bar, stripes that can move,
          stacked segments in their own colors, and an indeterminate
          runner; Term::Fabulous gets now and request_frame_at, with
          which widgets animate on the application's clock instead of
          timers of their own (Widget::Display/ANIMATION); a new manual
          page Term::Fabulous::Manual::Feedback
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::SegmentedControl: one choice of a few
          options shown side by side as one bar, with disabled segments,
          a vertical form, stretching to the layout's width, a hover
          look and KDL properties
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::StarRating: a rating of whole or half
          stars, chosen with the keys, a click or the wheel, with a hover
          preview, a value label, a read-only form and KDL properties
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Divider: a horizontal or vertical line
          between widgets, with an optional text at its start, center or
          end, in any border style or glyph and color; a vertical divider
          writes its text downwards. Term::Fabulous::Widget::Display, the
          new abstract base class of the widgets that paint themselves
          from their own state, holds the painting machinery the input
          widgets used to have; Term::Fabulous::Widget::Input derives
          from it
        - Documentation reviewed against the code and reorganized: the
          Manual is an index page with thirteen topic pages
          (Term::Fabulous::Manual::Layout, ::Looks, ::Events, ::Forms,
          ::Charts, ::Tables, ::TableRows, ::TableStyles, ::KDL,
          ::Programs, ::CustomWidgets, ::Troubleshooting, ::Glossary),
          the Cookbook an index of every recipe with fourteen topic
          pages, and the table guide moved from the Table class page to
          the three table pages of the Manual. Every page links to the
          previous and the next one; the Manual's feature index links
          every recipe. New example programs and screenshots for layout,
          text, colors, borders, events, KDL layout files, charts and
          tables; every screenshot is listed in Term::Fabulous::Examples
        - tools/screenshot and screenshots.kdl capture inline mode: the
          harness answers the cursor position query, and a scenario's
          shell lines are shown above the inline region
        - Chart widgets on top of Canvas: LineChart, AreaChart, BarChart
          and ScatterPlot (Widget::XYChart) with named series in several
          data forms, category, linear, logarithmic and time x axes with
          round ticks that land on rows, grid lines, axis titles and
          formats; grouped, stacked and percent-stacked bars and areas,
          stack groups, horizontal bars, value labels; curves (step,
          monotone, splines, easings), dashed and dotted lines, points,
          trend lines; Braille, block, half, quadrant, sextant and box
          rendering; transforms (normalize, index, cumulative, rate,
          moving average, exponential, median, gaussian, resample,
          downsample, regression, ...); from/to, span, max_points and
          append for live data. Histogram (automatic or given bins,
          count, percent, density, cumulative), Sparkline (one row, line,
          area or bar), PieChart, DonutChart (center text), PolarAreaChart
          and RadarChart. All charts (Widget::Chart) have a title, a
          legend at any side (with "+N more" when entries do not fit),
          palettes for dark and light backgrounds with chrome colors
          mixed from the background, and hover: the series under the
          pointer is emphasized and the new SeriesHover event tells the
          program what the pointer is on; KDL properties throughout.
          Supporting modules under Term::Fabulous::Chart. Documented in
          the widget PODs, a CHARTS chapter in the Manual and the
          Cookbook, ten gallery programs and eighteen cookbook programs;
          examples/showcase.pl draws its live chart with an AreaChart
        - Term::Fabulous::Widget::Table shows rows of Perl hashes in
          columns: widths that fit, grow, are fixed or a percentage,
          with limits; wrapping and rows as high as their tallest cell;
          mutators for the display text (Table::Mutator: dates, numbers,
          percentages, sizes, durations, flags, lookups); any widget as
          a cell, kept across data changes with update_cell; sorting by
          one or several columns by the user or from Perl, with string,
          natural, number, date or custom comparisons; filtering with a
          filter row whose expressions read text, numbers and date spans
          (">=100", "2024-05", "10..20"), named Table::Filter objects
          with all/any/not, on the raw value or the display text, and a
          search over the visible columns; groups by one or more
          columns with labels of your own; trees of nested rows; pages
          with a pager; a cursor and none, single or multiple selection
          with a selection column; a column chooser; colors, stripes and
          lines per table, column, row and cell, joined into one grid,
          and frames in the block styles Outer, Inner and Thick, which
          enclose colored cells cleanly;
          full keyboard and mouse use; KDL properties. User actions
          fire the new events CursorMove, SelectionChange, RowActivate,
          SortChange, FilterChange, PageChange, Expand, Collapse and
          ColumnsChange; changes from Perl fire none. The data and the
          view live in Table::Model, which has no widgets. Documented
          by feature with a feature index, a TABLES chapter in the
          Manual and the Cookbook, examples/widgets/table.pl,
          examples/table-directory.pl and examples/table-files.pl
        - Enum::BorderStyle junction gives the glyph where lines of any
          styles meet; Role::HasBorderStyle gains border_corners (corner
          glyphs of your own) and outer_border_sides (sides drawn on the
          background outside the widget), used by Render::Border
        - Text gains bold, italic and underline (also in KDL); the
          terminal grid emits italic
        - Render gains after_draw, a one-shot callback after the next
          frame. A change now costs one frame: the drawn revision is the
          one the layout started from, which removes a second frame
          after every interaction
        - Check gains sizing, which reads sizing strings such as
          'fit(0, 30)' or 'percent(25)'; KDL sizing and table column
          widths use it
        - Needs the matching Clay::UI (Grid spanning rows and shared
          columns, Preparable, laid_out_revision, scroll_state,
          scroll_to, bounding_box), List::Util 1.33 and Time::Local 1.30
        - Term::Fabulous::TextView lays out the text of the text
          inputs: visual rows with wrapping, the cursor's cell, the
          scroll position, the text position under a cell and moves by
          rows. TextField is a view of one row, TextArea one that may
          wrap; TextInput paints for both. Sideways scrolling has one
          rule for both: the view starts at a whole character of the
          cursor's line and scrolls no further than needed to fill it.
          TextInput's subclass interface shrinks to is_multi_line, view,
          paint, hides_text, display_cluster and apply_edit
          (scroll_to_cursor, position_at, clusters_between, columns_to,
          offset_at_column, paint_line_part, paint_placeholder and
          shows_placeholder are gone)
        - "Disabled" is Clay::UI's: the inputs, RadioGroup and Button
          compose Clay::UI::Role::Interaction::Disableable and
          Focusable directly, and Clay::UI derives can_focus, never
          presses a disabled widget and takes the focus away at once
          from a widget that is disabled or written can_focus(0). The
          derived state disabled shows in get_classes as
          state_disabled. Button gains disabled and disabled_color
          (also in KDL): a disabled button ignores clicks, Enter and
          Space and draws its border and its text in disabled_color.
          "Can this widget take the focus now" is asked in one place,
          Clay::UI's interaction->can_take_focus
        - Input widgets paint when a frame is drawn: setters record the
          value and call mark_changed, and the renderer calls the new
          Canvas hook refresh, where an input compares its paint key
          (its size, its own changes, focus, enabled, and what other
          widgets decide: a radio button's group, a dropdown list's
          colors and highlight, a text input's editor) and paints only
          when it changed. Fixes a disabled cursor button that left the
          focus look on the wrong radio button, an accent_color change
          that did not reach an open dropdown list, and a text field
          edited through $field->editor that stayed scrolled away from
          its cursor. Input->repaint, TextInput->cursor_moved,
          RadioGroup->repaint_buttons, Dropdown::List->repaint and the
          size_changed hook are gone; call mark_changed instead. A
          canvas reports a changed span only for cells that really
          changed, so clearing and drawing the same cells again sends
          nothing to the terminal. Editor->revision now counts changes
          of the text, the cursor and the selection; text_revision
          counts text changes
        - Widget properties are checked by one module,
          Term::Fabulous::Check, so every positive integer, string,
          boolean, single-column glyph and color is checked the same
          way and fails with one wording ("NAME must be a positive
          integer, got '0'"). Input colors are kept and returned as
          [r, g, b, a], like the colors of every widget
        - KDL properties are declared once per class:
          layout_properties is a class method returning name =>
          'scalar', 'boolean', 'color' or a parsing handler, and
          replaces boolean_layout_properties,
          structured_layout_properties, parse_node, parse_property and
          parse_generic; colors are declared, not recognized by a
          _color suffix. Term::Fabulous::Layout builds a widget with
          new( id => $id ) and then applies the layout node to the
          finished widget (apply_layout_node, apply_layout_settings);
          kdl_node is gone. Related values apply together in any order:
          a Slider's min, max and step (also new as set_range), a
          Dropdown's options before its value, a text input's
          max_length before its value
        - Term::Fabulous talks to the terminal only through a terminal
          object (Term::Fabulous::Role::Terminal), given to new as
          terminal: Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Termbox, the real terminal
          and the default (it also runs on other terminals through its
          input and output handles), or Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Memory,
          a terminal in memory for tests that queues keys by name
          (press_key), typed text, clicks, mouse events and resizes, and
          shows the screen as lines and cells. step is one turn of run
          without an event loop: it opens the terminal, reads and
          dispatches the queued input as run does, applies a resize at
          once and draws every due frame, returning how many; with
          paced => 1 and the new clock parameter it paces pointer motion
          like run. KeyPress->fields_for_name turns a key name back into
          termbox2's event fields. The renderer paints into one cell
          target object (cell_target) instead of seven target methods;
          Render::Target::Grid is a class that also formats its rows as
          text (row_text), and Render::Target::Termbox is replaced by
          Terminal::Termbox::Cells. The errors of opening the real
          terminal start with Term::Fabulous::Terminal::Termbox:
        - Term::Fabulous::Render::Frame works out once per frame what
          it paints: the paint order, the clip rect of every command
          and the cells every command paints. Drawing, canvas planning
          and hit-testing all read it. last_frame returns the Frame of
          the last frame (get_last_commands and get_last_clip_rects are
          gone), and it is complete even when painting died partway.
          Term::Fabulous::Render::Clip is gone; the painters' clip_rect
          comes from Term::Fabulous::Render. A border wider than one
          cell takes the mouse only on the cells it draws
        - Kitty keyboard protocol: run asks the terminal whether it
          speaks the protocol and switches it on (flags 1 and 4) until
          run returns; terminals without it are read as before, and
          Term::Fabulous->new( kitty_keyboard => 0 ) leaves it off.
          kitty_keyboard_active tells whether run uses it. Ctrl
          combinations get their own names (Ctrl+I, Ctrl+Shift+W,
          Ctrl+Enter), Alt+[ can be bound, the Super, Hyper and Meta
          modifiers and the keys F13 to F35, the keypad, the lock and
          media keys are reported. KeyPress gains main_key_name, which
          names the keypad keys after the main keyboard keys; the
          built-in widgets bind their keys with it.
          Term::Fabulous::Termbox gains tf_kitty_keyboard_query, the
          TF_KEY_* kitty keys and TF_MOD_SUPER, TF_MOD_HYPER and
          TF_MOD_META; its input parser decodes CSI u key reports and
          modified function keys with any modifier
        - Inline mode: Term::Fabulous->new( inline => $rows ) draws the
          user interface into that many rows below the shell's output
          instead of taking over the screen, draws the final state and
          keeps it there when run returns, and finds the region again
          after a resize. It has no mouse support; the cookbook recipe
          "Ask for input below the shell's output" shows it.
          Term::Fabulous::Termbox gains tf_init_inline,
          tf_init_inline_rwfd, tf_cursor_position, tf_reset_attrs and
          tf_flush
        - Dialogs keep the keyboard to themselves: keys the widgets of
          an open dialog do not use stop at its backdrop instead of
          reaching the key bindings behind it; when the focused widget
          inside is disabled or removed, the backdrop takes the focus;
          Shift+Tab from the backdrop goes to the last widget; close
          works (and fires Close) after the program took the dialog out
          of the tree; an open dialog no longer keeps a dropped UI
          alive. An open Dropdown list floats over every z_index, so
          it works inside a dialog
        - can_focus and disabled no longer depend on their order: an
          input or radio group built with disabled #true takes the
          focus once it is enabled, and can_focus written while
          disabled counts once enabled
        - Wheel scrolling is explicit: a widget that scrolled itself
          calls use_wheel on the Mouse event; what listeners return no
          longer keeps a ScrollBox from scrolling. TextArea, Slider and
          the Dropdown list leave a notch they cannot use to the
          ScrollBox around them
        - Mouse events say which button a release released
          (released_button); only the left button's release ends a
          press
        - run fires Start from inside the running loop ($ui->loop works
          in Start listeners), stops on SIGHUP too, gives back the
          program's INT, TERM and HUP handlers, and dies with "the
          terminal was closed" at the end of the terminal input instead
          of spinning
        - A click where a widget was removed since the last frame no
          longer kills run; a resize that ends at the old size repaints
          the canvases; borders beside an unchanged canvas keep their
          colors; pointer motion alone draws at most every other frame
          while frames are slow
        - TextArea wraps only the lines an edit changed; a wide
          character after a carried word is no longer hidden; the space
          at which a full row wraps hangs instead of starting the next
          row. Editor undo steps keep the replaced text only
        - Text inputs: Shift+click extends the selection; a TextField
          with a mask copies and cuts nothing and moves and deletes by
          the whole text instead of by word
        - PixelCanvas draw_line and draw_circle cost no more than the
          canvas is wide or high, however far the shape reaches
        - tb_send sends bytes as they are (tf_readable_bytes is new);
          Button takes #null and "reverse" in KDL; classes is checked
        - Documentation with pictures: Term::Fabulous::Examples lists
          every example program with a screenshot; the overview below
          the SYNOPSIS of Term::Fabulous (and in README.md) shows the
          new examples/showcase.pl; every widget page shows a preview
          below its SYNOPSIS, taken from the new widget gallery in
          examples/widgets; the Cookbook, the Manual and the pages of
          WebColor, BorderStyle and Static show the matching programs
        - The complete programs of Term::Fabulous::Cookbook are shipped
          in examples/cookbook, one file per recipe; the Cookbook's code
          blocks are copies of the shipped files
        - Maintainer tools in tools/ (shipped, not installed): make docs
          copies the example programs into the POD and takes the
          screenshots again, reproducibly (each program runs in a pseudo
          terminal on a virtual clock in a fixed environment); make
          docs-check fails when either is out of date and runs before
          make release; tools/screenshot takes an SVG or PNG screenshot
          of any Term::Fabulous program
        - Frames are drawn only when something changed: a widget setter
          (Clay::UI::Revision), Clay::XS::set_scroll_position, input,
          a resize or $ui->invalidate.
          Widget classes of your own call mark_changed (or repaint)
          after changing drawn state
        - The terminal reports mouse movement without a button (mode
          1003): MouseMove events, hover that follows the pointer, and
          Shift, Alt and Ctrl in Mouse events. A horizontal wheel
          scrolls sideways (TF_KEY_MOUSE_WHEEL_LEFT/RIGHT). The SGR
          mouse reports and Alt+key are decoded by Term::Fabulous's own
          parser (tf_install_input_parser), so Alt+x, Alt+Enter and
          Alt plus an umlaut are key names of their own
        - A Start event with the terminal size is fired on the root when
          run opens the terminal, before the first frame
        - Button fires Activate for a click and for Enter or Space,
          draws its border in focus_border_color while focused and
          inverts its colors while pressed (pressed_background_color)
        - Widget::Dialog opens a box centered over the screen behind a
          dimming backdrop, keeps Tab inside, closes on Escape and
          fires Close; it can be opened again. Requires Clay::UI with
          re-attachable widgets (a removed widget can be added again)
        - KDL layouts: sizing takes "grow(MIN)", "grow(MIN, MAX)",
          "fit(MIN)" and "fit(MIN, MAX)"; Box has the new property
          nodes child_alignment x=... y=... and floating attach_to=...
          parent_id=... element=... parent=... offset_x=... offset_y=...
          z_index=... pointer_capture=... clip_to=...; Text has
          wrap_mode and text_alignment; Button has focus_border_color
          and pressed_background_color
        - Every container widget composes
          Clay::UI::Role::Layout::HasFloating and accepts a floating
          parameter and accessor
        - Widget::find_by_id and $ui->find_by_id find a widget (Text
          included) by its id, depth first
        - max_element_count raises Clay's element limit (8192 by
          default); a too-large tree dies with a message that names it
        - Border styles: Tab is gone (it was identical to Wide).
          Hidden now switches a side off: it draws nothing and takes
          no space, whatever the side's border_width; Blank still
          takes space and draws spaces
        - First version. Terminal UIs from Clay layouts, drawn with
          termbox2: Box, Text and Button widgets, border styles,
          truecolor rendering, KDL layout files and an IO::Async
          driven event loop with key, mouse and resize events
        - Term::Fabulous::Static renders a widget tree once, to text
          with optional ANSI truecolor, without opening the terminal.
          The render roles paint through a cell target role
          (Render::Target::Termbox for the terminal, Render::Target::Grid
          for memory) instead of calling termbox2 directly
        - termbox2 is compiled into the distribution as
          Term::Fabulous::Termbox (XS, 64-bit attributes, grapheme
          clusters); the Termbox and FFI::Platypus dependencies are
          gone. Text is measured with termbox2's own width functions,
          so emoji presentation sequences (U+FE0F), ZWJ sequences and
          flags take two columns
        - Term::Fabulous::Unicode remembers the grapheme clusters and
          the width of every text it has measured, so layout callbacks
          and drawing no longer segment the same strings on every frame
        - Scroll containers: the renderer honors Clay's scissor commands
          (Render::Clip), Widget::ScrollBox is a Box that scrolls, and
          the mouse wheel scrolls the box under the pointer by three
          rows per notch. Hit testing ignores content scrolled out of
          view
        - Tab and Shift-Tab move the keyboard focus after their KeyPress
        - Requires the Clay::UI interaction tracker: focus is read
          through $ui->interaction, and border_width is validated by
          Clay::UI when it is set
        - Widget::Canvas: a free-drawing buffer of grapheme clusters
          with any foreground and background color, sized by the layout
          (CanvasResize event). Frames keep the cells of canvases that
          did not move and that nothing covers (Render::Target::Mask),
          so only their changed cells are painted
        - Widget::PixelCanvas: a canvas of half-block pixels, two per
          cell, with pixel, rect, line and circle drawing. Canvas::cell_at
          and PixelCanvas::pixel_at map mouse events to cells and pixels
        - Input widgets: TextField, TextArea, Checkbox, RadioGroup with
          RadioButton, Dropdown and Slider, built on Widget::Input (a
          canvas that paints itself, takes the focus and sizes itself to
          its content) and fired Change events; TextField fires Submit.
          The text inputs share Term::Fabulous::Editor: grapheme-aware
          cursor movement, selection, word editing keys, undo and redo,
          and a clipboard. All of them can be built from KDL layouts
        - A left mouse press focuses the widget under the pointer (or its
          nearest focusable ancestor) and blurs when there is none
        - Clay gets the pointer at the center of its cell, so pressing the
          top row of a widget no longer also presses the widget above it
        - Event::KeyPress::key_name and text name keys for bindings
          ('Ctrl+Shift+Left', 'Enter', 'a'); Widget::Box::parse_property
          lets subclasses parse KDL properties of their own
        - Documentation: Term::Fabulous::Manual (user guide) and
          Term::Fabulous::Cookbook (recipes), reference documentation
          for every module, and the examples buttons-and-keys.pl,
          kdl-form.pl and custom-widget.pl
        - Translucent backgrounds: a background_color with an alpha from
          1 to 254 is blended with what lies below the widget. The new
          widget parameter glyphs_show_through decides whether the text
          and borders below stay visible, tinted, or are covered. The
          cell targets gained painted_cell to read a cell back
        - Term::Fabulous::Enum::WebColor holds the 148 CSS named colors
          as Term::Fabulous::Color objects (it was an empty placeholder)
        - Every widget color (background_color, border_color, the
          text_color of a Text widget) takes any Term::Fabulous::Color
          input, and Color->new reads a packed 0xRRGGBB integer. The
          rgba and hsla class methods read alpha like the string forms,
          and blend validates its arguments and rounds. KDL boolean
          properties accept only #true/#false (or 0/1), a second
          padding node merges, and the "unknown layout property" error
          lists the structured properties too. Boolean parameters are
          stored as 1 or 0, checkbox and radio labels stay in place when
          the mark changes, Static rejects unknown options, the
          constructors reject measure_text, key_name has no name for C1
          control characters, editor errors name the text input, and
          the editor clipboard can be read through an editor
        - Text widgets take character strings like everything else;
          UTF-8 bytes no longer have to be encoded for them. can_focus
          given to new is honored by the input widgets and RadioGroup,
          and disabled(0) restores it. A press and a release within one
          frame both reach Clay, so fast clicks press buttons. A wheel
          notch a widget handled (TextArea, Slider, the dropdown list)
          no longer also scrolls the ScrollBox around it; fire_event
          reports the outcome for that. A border_style_<side> parameter
          wins over border_style, as in KDL. Slider values keep the
          decimals of min and of tiny steps. TextInput drops its
          Shift+click branch, which termbox2 can never trigger, and
          examples/kdl-layout.pl starts again
